IMPORT prelude

-- Postal Services Act 1999 (Singapore) -- what a "letter" is (s 2(2)), the Postal Authority's letter monopoly and its exceptions
-- (ss 4, 5, 33), postage stamps bearing "Singapore" (s 19), interfering with mail and with parcels taken from public parcel
-- lockers (ss 36, 39I), prohibited items in public parcel lockers (s 39J), rival parcel lockers on specified premises (ss 23A,
-- 39G), the penalties (ss 29(7), 31, 34(2), 39E, 39H(2), 52), composition (s 51) and the licensee's exclusion of liability (s 57).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/PSA1999.txt. The edition
-- says it incorporates amendments up to 1 December 2021; later amendments are annotated in the deposit, the latest being Act 15
-- of 2026 (wef 1 July 2026, strata "common property" definitions), alongside Act 30 of 2021 (wef 2 November 2022) and Act 25 of
-- 2021 (wef 1 April 2022). THE ARRANGEMENT OF SECTIONS AT THE TOP OF THE DEPOSIT IS OUT OF STEP WITH THE BODY (it lists
-- "Postage stamps" as s 18; the body has it as s 19). Section numbers here follow the body.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Cited by 8 of the 527 Singapore Acts deposited in this repository. SCOPED to the rules an ordinary sender,
-- courier, building owner or parcel-locker user meets. Not encoded: the Postal Authority's functions and powers (Part 1A),
-- licensing, designation, modification and cancellation (ss 6-10A), the installation and entry powers for public parcel lockers
-- (ss 23F-23R), codes of practice and directions (Part 4), control of designated postal licensees (Part 4A), international
-- obligations (Part 6), enforcement powers (Part 7 except ss 51, 52), and the offences in ss 27, 28, 30, 34(1), 35, 38, 39,
-- 39A-39D and 39H(1) beyond their penalty where one is listed.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill conventions (skill not loadable in this session;
-- the conventions of the example rows were followed). No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate.
-- See NOTES.md.

§ `Postal Services Act 1999 -- offences and the letter monopoly`

§§ `2(2) -- what is a letter`

-- s 2(2): "letter" means "any communication in written form on any kind of physical medium (not exceeding 500 grams in weight)
-- to be conveyed and delivered (otherwise than electronically) to a particular addressee or address indicated by the sender ...
-- but does not include any book, catalogue, newspaper or periodical."
DECLARE Item HAS
    `is a written communication` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is on a physical medium` IS A BOOLEAN
    `weight in grams` IS A NUMBER
    `is conveyed electronically` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is addressed to a particular addressee or address` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is a book, catalogue, newspaper or periodical` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the item` IS AN Item
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the item is a letter` IF
        `the item`'s `is a written communication`
    AND `the item`'s `is on a physical medium`
    AND `the item`'s `weight in grams` AT MOST 500
    AND (NOT `the item`'s `is conveyed electronically`)
    AND `the item`'s `is addressed to a particular addressee or address`
    AND (NOT `the item`'s `is a book, catalogue, newspaper or periodical`)

§§ `4, 5 and 33 -- the letter monopoly`

-- s 4(1): the Postal Authority "has the exclusive privilege of conveying from one place to another letters" and the incidental
-- services. (2): no one else may do so unless the Postal Authority, a postal licensee, or an employee or agent of either.
-- s 5(1): the privilege is NOT infringed by (a) "letters not exceeding 3 in number sent by a person for delivery by the person to
-- another without hire, reward or other profit"; (b) a letter "solely concerning the affairs of either correspondent by an
-- employee of either correspondent"; (c) a "Consignee's letter" carried with the goods, without reward, open to inspection; (d)
-- direct mail, a letter carried by the sender personally, or a document whose service otherwise than by post is required or
-- authorised by written law; (e) an intra-organisational mailroom service or a messenger service.
-- s 33: "Subject to section 5", conveying a letter, collecting or delivering it to be sent, or making a collection of letters
-- for transmission through a postal licensee, "without a licence granted under section 6" is an offence. No penalty is expressed,
-- so s 52 applies.
DECLARE Conveyance HAS
    `number of letters` IS A NUMBER
    `is for hire, reward or profit` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is by an employee of a correspondent about that correspondent's affairs` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is an open consignee's letter carried with the goods` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is direct mail` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is by the sender personally` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is a document another law lets be served otherwise than by post` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is an intra-organisational mailroom service` IS A BOOLEAN
    `is a messenger service` IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN `the conveyance` IS A Conveyance
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a section 5 exception applies` IF
        (`the conveyance`'s `number of letters` AT MOST 3 AND (NOT `the conveyance`'s `is for hire, reward or profit`))
    OR  `the conveyance`'s `is by an employee of a correspondent about that correspondent's affairs`
    OR  (`the conveyance`'s `is an open consignee's letter carried with the goods` AND (NOT `the conveyance`'s `is for hire, reward or profit`))
    OR  `the conveyance`'s `is direct mail`
    OR  `the conveyance`'s `is by the sender personally`
    OR  `the conveyance`'s `is a document another law lets be served otherwise than by post`
    OR  `the conveyance`'s `is an intra-organisational mailroom service`
    OR  `the conveyance`'s `is a messenger service`

-- `authorised under s 4(2)` covers the Postal Authority, a postal licensee and their employees and agents. s 33 itself speaks only
-- of acting "without a licence granted under section 6"; treating the Postal Authority and its staff as outside s 33 is an
-- INFERENCE from s 4(2).
GIVEN `the conveyance` IS A Conveyance, `authorised under s 4(2)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `conveying the letters is an offence under s 33` IF
        (NOT `authorised under s 4(2)`)
    AND (NOT `a section 5 exception applies` `the conveyance`)

§§ `19 -- postage stamps bearing "Singapore"`

-- s 19(1): a person "(other than the Postal Authority or a specified public postal licensee) must not provide or cause to be
-- provided postage stamps that bear the word 'Singapore' in any language". (9): a specified public postal licensee is one whose
-- s 6 licence authorises it to do so. (2): contravention is an offence (penalty: s 52).
GIVEN `bears the word Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN, `is the Postal Authority` IS A BOOLEAN, `is a licensee authorised to provide such stamps` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `providing the stamps is an offence under s 19` IF
        `bears the word Singapore`
    AND (NOT `is the Postal Authority`)
    AND (NOT `is a licensee authorised to provide such stamps`)

§§ `36 and 39I -- keeping or opening someone else's mail or parcel`

-- s 36(1)(a): wilfully retaining, secreting, making away with or detaining a postal article in the course of transmission by post;
-- (b) wilfully opening "any letter which ought to have been delivered", or impeding its due delivery -- each "except in accordance
-- with this Act or unless authorised by the Postal Authority". (2): "Nothing in subsection (1)(b)" applies to a parent or guardian
-- of a minor or ward addressee. The parent exception does NOT reach (1)(a), and s 36 has no exception for the addressee's consent.
DECLARE MailAct IS ONE OF
    `retaining a postal article in transmission`
    `opening a letter that ought to have been delivered`
    `impeding the due delivery of a letter`

GIVEN `the act` IS A MailAct, `authorised by the Act or the Postal Authority` IS A BOOLEAN, `is parent or guardian of a minor or ward addressee` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the act is an offence under s 36` IF
        (NOT `authorised by the Act or the Postal Authority`)
    AND CONSIDER `the act`
        WHEN `retaining a postal article in transmission` THEN TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOT `is parent or guardian of a minor or ward addressee`

-- s 39I(1): a person "(not being the intended recipient)" who, except in accordance with the Act or unless authorised by the
-- Postal Authority, wilfully retains, keeps, detains or opens "any parcel that had been collected from a public parcel locker".
-- (2): not an offence where (a) "the intended recipient of the parcel authorises the individual", or (b) the individual is a
-- parent or guardian of a minor or ward recipient.
GIVEN `is the intended recipient` IS A BOOLEAN, `authorised by the Act or the Postal Authority` IS A BOOLEAN, `authorised by the intended recipient` IS A BOOLEAN, `is parent or guardian of a minor or ward recipient` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `keeping or opening the parcel is an offence under s 39I` IF
        (NOT `is the intended recipient`)
    AND (NOT `authorised by the Act or the Postal Authority`)
    AND (NOT `authorised by the intended recipient`)
    AND (NOT `is parent or guardian of a minor or ward recipient`)

§§ `39J -- prohibited items in a public parcel locker`

-- s 39J(1): placing or causing to be placed a prohibited item in a public parcel locker is an offence. (2): not where the person
-- "does not know, and has no reason to believe, that the thing is or contains a prohibited item". (3): prohibited items include
-- indecent or obscene parcels, things likely to damage the locker or injure users, and (subject to regulations) explosive,
-- dangerous or noxious substances, unprotected sharp instruments and noxious living creatures.
GIVEN `the thing is a prohibited item` IS A BOOLEAN, `knew or had reason to believe` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `placing the thing is an offence under s 39J` IF
    `the thing is a prohibited item` AND `knew or had reason to believe`

§§ `23A and 39G -- rival parcel lockers on specified premises`

-- s 23A: "specified premises" means (i) "the common property of a building in an HDB housing estate"; (ii) a bus interchange;
-- (iii) a railway commuter facility or railway premises; (iv) a People's Association community centre or club; (v) premises
-- prescribed by the Minister by order in the Gazette; and includes adjoining State land.
-- s 39G(1): a person "(other than the public parcel locker network operator) that provides or operates, or offers to provide or
-- operate, a parcel locker in or on any specified premises shall be guilty of an offence".
DECLARE Place IS ONE OF
    `HDB common property`
    `a bus interchange`
    `an MRT station`
    `a People's Association community centre`
    `condominium common property`
    `a shopping mall`

GIVEN `the place` IS A Place, `prescribed by Ministerial order` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the place is specified premises` IF
        `prescribed by Ministerial order`
    OR  CONSIDER `the place`
        WHEN `condominium common property` THEN FALSE
        WHEN `a shopping mall` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE

GIVEN `the place` IS A Place, `prescribed by Ministerial order` IS A BOOLEAN, `is the public parcel locker network operator` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `operating a parcel locker there is an offence under s 39G` IF
        (NOT `is the public parcel locker network operator`)
    AND `the place is specified premises` `the place` `prescribed by Ministerial order`

§§ `29(7), 31, 34(2), 39E, 39H(2) and 52 -- penalties`

-- s 52: an offence "for which no penalty is expressly provided" -- a fine up to $10,000 or imprisonment up to 3 years or both,
-- and for a continuing offence a further fine up to $1,000 "for every day or part of a day" after conviction.
-- s 31: wilfully removing, destroying or damaging a public postal licensee's installation or plant -- $50,000 or 3 years.
-- s 39E: the same for a public parcel locker, related installation or related software -- $50,000 or 3 years.
-- s 34(2): a licensee's officer fraudulently marking a postal article or demanding unchargeable money -- $5,000 or 12 months.
-- s 39H(2): a specified person defacing a parcel's identifying mark -- $5,000 or 12 months.
-- s 29(7): placards on or disfiguring postal plant, or laying wires likely to damage it, without written approval -- a fine up to
-- $10,000 (no imprisonment) and $1,000 a day continuing.
DECLARE Offence IS ONE OF
    `unlicensed conveyance of letters`
    `providing Singapore stamps`
    `retaining or opening mail`
    `sending dangerous things by post`
    `placing a prohibited item in a locker`
    `operating a rival parcel locker`
    `damaging postal plant`
    `damaging a public parcel locker`
    `fraudulent official mark by a postal officer`
    `defacing a parcel's identifying mark`
    `disfiguring postal plant`

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `damaging postal plant` THEN 50000
    WHEN `damaging a public parcel locker` THEN 50000
    WHEN `fraudulent official mark by a postal officer` THEN 5000
    WHEN `defacing a parcel's identifying mark` THEN 5000
    OTHERWISE 10000

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum imprisonment in months` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the offence`
    WHEN `disfiguring postal plant` THEN 0
    WHEN `fraudulent official mark by a postal officer` THEN 12
    WHEN `defacing a parcel's identifying mark` THEN 12
    OTHERWISE 36

GIVEN `days continuing after conviction` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum further fine for a continuing offence` MEANS 1000 TIMES `days continuing after conviction`

§§ `51 -- composition`

-- s 51(1): a specially authorised officer may compound a prescribed compoundable offence by collecting a sum "not exceeding the
-- lower of" (a) $5,000 and (b) "one half the maximum fine prescribed for that offence". Which offences are compoundable is left
-- to regulations (s 51(3)); none were read.
GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lower of` MEANS IF `a` AT MOST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

GIVEN `the offence` IS AN Offence
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum composition sum` MEANS `the lower of` 5000 (`the maximum fine` `the offence` DIVIDED BY 2)

§§ `57 -- exclusion of the public postal licensee's liability`

-- s 57(1): a public postal licensee "shall not be liable" for injury, loss or damage by reason of (a) loss, misdelivery, delay or
-- damage of a postal article in transmission; (b)-(c) failure, delay or interruption of service; (d) loss of secrecy; (e) wrong
-- or delayed payment of a remittance -- "which is due to the act or default of another person, or an accident or some other cause
-- beyond the control of the public postal licensee". (2): it may still pay an indemnity under the Universal Postal Union
-- Convention for parcels and insured or registered articles. The section does not exclude liability for the licensee's own default.
DECLARE Cause IS ONE OF
    `the licensee's own default`
    `another person's act or default`
    `an accident`
    `another cause beyond the licensee's control`

GIVEN `the loss is of a listed kind` IS A BOOLEAN, `the cause` IS A Cause
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 57 excludes the licensee's liability` IF
        `the loss is of a listed kind`
    AND CONSIDER `the cause`
        WHEN `the licensee's own default` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE
